INT. USED BOOKSTORE - POETRY ROOM - EVENING

A narrow room at the back of a sprawling shop, shelves bowed with paperbacks. A podium, a lamp, and fifteen folding chairs, most of them empty. A handwritten sign: OPEN MIC - POETRY - 7 PM.

MARISOL VEGA-RHODES, 30s, in a green coat, stands at the podium with a folded page. In the front row, alone, sits ELIAS THORNBURY, 30s, a bookseller in an apron, holding a pencil he has forgotten to use.

MARISOL
(to the room)
Thank you all for coming.

There is no one else. She looks at the empty chairs. She smiles at them anyway.

MARISOL (CONT'D)
This is called "Interlibrary Loan."

She unfolds the page. Elias leans forward.

MARISOL (CONT'D)
(reading)
I borrowed you from a stranger's shelf
and kept you longer than I should.
The due date came, the due date went,
and still I read you like a wood.

Elias's pencil stops. She reads the next stanza quietly, glancing at him once, twice.

MARISOL (CONT'D)
(reading)
You were stamped by someone else, in blue.
Their name is smudged, their initials blurred.
I'd like to think they'd understand
how long I held on to the word.

She finishes. Folds the page. A silence. Elias claps, slowly, three times.

ELIAS
That was good.

MARISOL
Thank you.

ELIAS
Is it about a book?

MARISOL
It might be.

ELIAS
It sounds like it's about a person.

MARISOL
It might be that too.

He stands. Crosses the aisle. Stops three feet from the podium.

ELIAS
Can I ask who?

MARISOL
You can ask.

ELIAS
Who?

MARISOL
You work here, don't you?

ELIAS
Yes.

MARISOL
Then you should check your records. I've had a copy of "Collected Neruda" out for two years.

ELIAS
We don't lend books.

MARISOL
I know.

He looks at her. She looks at the empty chairs, then at him.

ELIAS
(quietly)
Marisol.

MARISOL
Elias.

ELIAS
It's a used bookstore.

MARISOL
Yes.

ELIAS
You're not supposed to give them back.

MARISOL
I never meant to.

He takes the pencil from behind his ear and puts it in his apron pocket. He reaches out, and she lets him take the page. He unfolds it, reads the last line, and folds it again.

ELIAS
I close at nine.

MARISOL
I know.

ELIAS
I'm not busy at nine.

MARISOL
Neither am I.

He tucks the poem into his apron. She steps down from the podium. Together they turn off the lamp and leave fifteen chairs in the dark.
